=\"none\" height=\"103\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\" width=\"274.80555725097656\" x=\"70.09722137451172\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" y=\"380.590576171875\"></rect><rect fill=\"#666666\" height=\"12\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\" width=\"12\" x=\"77.09722137451172\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" y=\"392.590576171875\"></rect><text fill=\"#000000\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\" font-size=\"19.84\" text-anchor=\"top\" transform=\"translate(99.09722137451172 404.590576171875)\"> tax and public spending policy</text><rect fill=\"#CCCCCC\" height=\"12\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\" width=\"12\" x=\"77.09722137451172\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" y=\"424.590576171875\"></rect><text fill=\"#000000\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\" font-size=\"19.84\" text-anchor=\"top\" transform=\"translate(99.09722137451172 436.590576171875)\"> trade policy</text><rect fill=\"#000000\" height=\"12\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\" width=\"12\" x=\"77.09722137451172\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" y=\"456.590576171875\"></rect><text fill=\"#000000\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\" font-size=\"19.84\" text-anchor=\"top\" transform=\"translate(99.09722137451172 468.590576171875)\"> general economic policy</text></g></svg></figure><div aria-label=\"Long description for bar graph titled Economic Policy Uncertainty in the United Kingdom, 2005 to 2010\" class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\"><ul>\n<li>For each data category, the following bars are shown:\n<ul>\n<li>Tax and public spending policy</li>\n<li>Trade policy</li>\n<li>General economic policy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The data for the 6 categories are as follows:\n<ul>\n<li>2005:\n<ul>\n<li>Tax and public spending policy: 98</li>\n<li>Trade policy: 160</li>\n<li>General economic policy: 90</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2006:\n<ul>\n<li>Tax and public spending policy: 65</li>\n<li>Trade policy: 105</li>\n<li>General economic policy: 72</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2007:\n<ul>\n<li>Tax and public spending policy: 61</li>\n<li>Trade policy: 75</li>\n<li>General economic policy: 77</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2008:\n<ul>\n<li>Tax and public spending policy: 90</li>\n<li>Trade policy: 71</li>\n<li>General economic policy: 73</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2009:\n<ul>\n<li>Tax and public spending policy: 76</li>\n<li>Trade policy: 70</li>\n<li>General economic policy: 81</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2010:\n<ul>\n<li>Tax and public spending policy: 165</li>\n<li>Trade policy: 71</li>\n<li>General economic policy: 118</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>High levels of public uncertainty about which economic policies a country will adopt can make planning difficult for businesses, but measures of such uncertainty have not tended to be very detailed. Recently, however, economist Sandile Hlatshwayo analyzed trends in news reports to derive measures not only for general economic policy uncertainty but also for uncertainty related to specific areas of economic policy, like tax or trade policy. One revelation of her work is that a general measure may not fully reflect uncertainty about specific areas of policy, as in the case of the United Kingdom, where general economic policy uncertainty <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to illustrate the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2bce906c-f71f-43ab-8e56-6669a33e0ba1","content":"<p>aligned closely with uncertainty about tax and public spending policy in 2005 but differed from uncertainty about tax and public spending policy by a large amount in 2009.</p>"},{"id":"fe2dce73-ec8c-4ea5-8c68-04735cd031dc","content":"<p>was substantially lower than uncertainty about tax and public spending policy each year from 2005 to 2010.</p>"},{"id":"9d96e614-f847-4b56-8c30-4411daeab130","content":"<p>reached its highest level between 2005 and 2010 in the same year that uncertainty about trade policy and tax and public spending policy reached their lowest levels.</p>"},{"id":"b7167200-dbc4-40fe-b74d-283af542cedf","content":"<p>was substantially lower than uncertainty about trade policy in 2005 and substantially higher than uncertainty about trade policy in 2010.</p>"}],"keys":["b7167200-dbc4-40fe-b74d-283af542cedf"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it uses data from the graph to effectively illustrate the text&rsquo;s claim about general economic policy uncertainty in the United Kingdom. The graph presents values for economic policy uncertainty in tax and public spending policy, trade policy, and general economic policy in the UK from 2005 to 2010. The graph shows that in 2005, the value for general economic policy uncertainty (approximately 90) was substantially lower than the value for uncertainty about trade policy specifically (approximately 160). It also shows that in 2010, the value for general economic policy uncertainty (approximately 120) was substantially higher than the value for uncertainty about trade policy (approximately 70). The substantial differences between these values in 2005 and 2010 support the claim that a general measure may not fully reflect uncertainty about specific areas of policy. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the graph shows that the level of general economic policy uncertainty was similar to the level of uncertainty about tax and public spending policy in both 2005 (with values of approximately 90 and 100, respectively) and 2009 (with values of approximately 80 and 75, respectively).&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because the graph shows that general economic policy uncertainty was higher than uncertainty about tax and public spending policy in 2006, 2007, and 2009, not that it was lower each year from 2005 to 2010. Choice C is incorrect because the graph shows that general economic policy uncertainty reached its highest level in 2010, which was when uncertainty about tax and public spending policy also reached its highest level, not its lowest level. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-507-QUA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"82ccd593-567d-4eba-89d8-e478bd8b0fb1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959337,"updateDate":1691007959337}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959337, 1691007959337),
    ($SATQ$706046f7$SATQ$, $SATQ$77a2ee2e-2bee-4a40-a24b-c3515b3bb5ae$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the 1960s, Gloria Richardson led a movement to promote racial equality. Her involvement in this effort was inspired by her daughter, Donna Richardson. In 1961, Donna joined protests organized by the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee in Cambridge, Maryland. Following her daughter, Gloria joined these protests too. Gloria soon became the cochair of the Cambridge Nonviolent Action Committee. She was also the leader of what became known as the Cambridge movement.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the text, what did Gloria Richardson lead?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"14f5995a-d653-449c-b4eb-394f93903705","content":"<p>The Cambridge movement</p>"},{"id":"1ad3db09-ee7a-46aa-9804-7f50bc2908e3","content":"<p>Her daughter Donna&rsquo;s high school</p>"},{"id":"8b0eaf75-af8c-400d-8793-d37a4fecf5d6","content":"<p>Protests to support environmental protections</p>"},{"id":"659150a6-2dae-4735-8307-2582f6f41193","content":"<p>A new business in Cambridge, Maryland</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["14f5995a-d653-449c-b4eb-394f93903705"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents information about Gloria Richardson that is supported by the text. The text provides a number of details about Gloria&rsquo;s involvement in efforts to promote racial equality, including that she was the leader of what became known as the Cambridge movement.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text never indicates that Gloria Richardson led her daughter Donna&rsquo;s high school. The text says only that Gloria was inspired by her daughter to become involved in efforts to promote racial equality. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention protests related to environmental protections. Rather, the text discusses Gloria Richardson&rsquo;s involvement in efforts to promote racial equality. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Gloria Richardson led a new business in Cambridge, Maryland. Rather, the text states that she led what became known as the Cambridge movement.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"706046f7","external_id":"77a2ee2e-2bee-4a40-a24b-c3515b3bb5ae","vaultid":null,"uId":"ded3c986-40de-42ef-94fc-d1a111b83244","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the 1960s, Gloria Richardson led a movement to promote racial equality. Her involvement in this effort was inspired by her daughter, Donna Richardson. In 1961, Donna joined protests organized by the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee in Cambridge, Maryland. Following her daughter, Gloria joined these protests too. Gloria soon became the cochair of the Cambridge Nonviolent Action Committee. She was also the leader of what became known as the Cambridge movement.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>According to the text, what did Gloria Richardson lead?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"14f5995a-d653-449c-b4eb-394f93903705","content":"<p>The Cambridge movement</p>"},{"id":"1ad3db09-ee7a-46aa-9804-7f50bc2908e3","content":"<p>Her daughter Donna&rsquo;s high school</p>"},{"id":"8b0eaf75-af8c-400d-8793-d37a4fecf5d6","content":"<p>Protests to support environmental protections</p>"},{"id":"659150a6-2dae-4735-8307-2582f6f41193","content":"<p>A new business in Cambridge, Maryland</p>"}],"keys":["14f5995a-d653-449c-b4eb-394f93903705"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents information about Gloria Richardson that is supported by the text. The text provides a number of details about Gloria&rsquo;s involvement in efforts to promote racial equality, including that she was the leader of what became known as the Cambridge movement.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text never indicates that Gloria Richardson led her daughter Donna&rsquo;s high school. The text says only that Gloria was inspired by her daughter to become involved in efforts to promote racial equality. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention protests related to environmental protections. Rather, the text discusses Gloria Richardson&rsquo;s involvement in efforts to promote racial equality. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Gloria Richardson led a new business in Cambridge, Maryland. Rather, the text states that she led what became known as the Cambridge movement.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669209,"updateDate":1743430669209}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669209, 1743430669209),
    ($SATQ$70aacc03$SATQ$, $SATQ$c62128d3-85ec-4b5a-8f7e-cedfc5850c29$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Elizabeth Asiedu has identified a negative correlation between the share of developing countries&rsquo; economies derived from natural-resource extraction and those countries&rsquo; receipts of foreign investment. This may appear counterintuitive&mdash;resource extraction requires initial investments (in extractive technology, for instance) at scales best met by multinational corporations&mdash;but Asiedu notes that natural-resource industries&rsquo; boom-bust cycle can destabilize local currencies and increase developing countries&rsquo; vulnerability to external shocks, creating levels of uncertainty to which foreign investors are typically averse.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2059d26c-45a2-40b8-ac22-2f37da0ea508","content":"<p>Although it may seem surprising that foreign investment declines in developing countries as natural-resource extraction makes up a larger share of those countries&rsquo; economies, that decline happens because resource extraction requires initial investments too large for foreign investors to supply.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"062cbc77-4d0f-4aee-a7d7-4bbdbfe11fbb","content":"<p>Although developing countries tend to become less dependent on foreign investment as natural-resource industries make up a larger share of their economies, this change may not occur if the boom-bust cycle of those industries destabilizes local currencies or increases countries&rsquo; vulnerability to external shocks.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"fb642109-7216-4692-860f-670ef46dec44","content":"<p>Although one might expect that foreign investment would increase as natural-resource extraction makes up a larger share of developing countries&rsquo; economies, the opposite happens because heavy reliance on natural resources can lead to unattractive conditions for investors.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2b6d19f5-31b3-4cbd-b10e-1996db8ae2f8","content":"<p>Although foreign investors tend to avoid initial investments in natural-resource industries in developing countries, foreign investment may increase significantly as those industries stabilize and the risks associated with them decline.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fb642109-7216-4692-860f-670ef46dec44"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it accurately states the main idea of the text. According to the text, contrary to what some might expect, foreign investment is typically lower in developing countries whose economies are more dependent on natural-resource extraction. The text explains that high reliance on natural-resource extraction can subject a developing country to economic shocks that can destabilize the local currency and introduce economic uncertainty that tends to keep investors away. In other words, although we may think otherwise, foreign investors are less willing to invest in projects in developing countries whose economies are heavily dependent on natural-resource extraction because those economies tend to exhibit instability that investors want to avoid.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text does indicate that foreign investment is typically lower in developing countries whose economies are more dependent on natural-resource extraction; the text further indicates that natural-resource extraction requires substantial initial investments (to acquire things like required technologies) for which there are fewer investors willing to participate at this stage than one might think. But the text does not implicate the cost of these initial investments as a reason why foreign investment is less widely available than some might think. Choice B is incorrect. The text indicates that greater dependence on natural-resource extraction makes a developing country less appealing to foreign investors because of associated economic instability. Rather than arguing that the goal of developing countries is to become less dependent on foreign investment, as the phrasing of choice B suggests, the text focuses only on why foreign investors become less involved with such countries, which suggests that more investment would be preferable. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text indicates that natural-resource extraction requires substantial initial investments (to acquire things like required technologies) and that there are fewer likely investors willing to participate at this stage than one might think, the text does not address what investors are likely to do over time as the industry stabilizes itself.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"70aacc03","external_id":"c62128d3-85ec-4b5a-8f7e-cedfc5850c29","vaultid":null,"uId":"37d3b001-cfd9-4346-ace7-c8a4bb15118d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Elizabeth Asiedu has identified a negative correlation between the share of developing countries&rsquo; economies derived from natural-resource extraction and those countries&rsquo; receipts of foreign investment. This may appear counterintuitive&mdash;resource extraction requires initial investments (in extractive technology, for instance) at scales best met by multinational corporations&mdash;but Asiedu notes that natural-resource industries&rsquo; boom-bust cycle can destabilize local currencies and increase developing countries&rsquo; vulnerability to external shocks, creating levels of uncertainty to which foreign investors are typically averse.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2059d26c-45a2-40b8-ac22-2f37da0ea508","content":"<p>Although it may seem surprising that foreign investment declines in developing countries as natural-resource extraction makes up a larger share of those countries&rsquo; economies, that decline happens because resource extraction requires initial investments too large for foreign investors to supply.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"062cbc77-4d0f-4aee-a7d7-4bbdbfe11fbb","content":"<p>Although developing countries tend to become less dependent on foreign investment as natural-resource industries make up a larger share of their economies, this change may not occur if the boom-bust cycle of those industries destabilizes local currencies or increases countries&rsquo; vulnerability to external shocks.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"fb642109-7216-4692-860f-670ef46dec44","content":"<p>Although one might expect that foreign investment would increase as natural-resource extraction makes up a larger share of developing countries&rsquo; economies, the opposite happens because heavy reliance on natural resources can lead to unattractive conditions for investors.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2b6d19f5-31b3-4cbd-b10e-1996db8ae2f8","content":"<p>Although foreign investors tend to avoid initial investments in natural-resource industries in developing countries, foreign investment may increase significantly as those industries stabilize and the risks associated with them decline.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["fb642109-7216-4692-860f-670ef46dec44"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it accurately states the main idea of the text. According to the text, contrary to what some might expect, foreign investment is typically lower in developing countries whose economies are more dependent on natural-resource extraction. The text explains that high reliance on natural-resource extraction can subject a developing country to economic shocks that can destabilize the local currency and introduce economic uncertainty that tends to keep investors away. In other words, although we may think otherwise, foreign investors are less willing to invest in projects in developing countries whose economies are heavily dependent on natural-resource extraction because those economies tend to exhibit instability that investors want to avoid.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text does indicate that foreign investment is typically lower in developing countries whose economies are more dependent on natural-resource extraction; the text further indicates that natural-resource extraction requires substantial initial investments (to acquire things like required technologies) for which there are fewer investors willing to participate at this stage than one might think. But the text does not implicate the cost of these initial investments as a reason why foreign investment is less widely available than some might think. Choice B is incorrect. The text indicates that greater dependence on natural-resource extraction makes a developing country less appealing to foreign investors because of associated economic instability. Rather than arguing that the goal of developing countries is to become less dependent on foreign investment, as the phrasing of choice B suggests, the text focuses only on why foreign investors become less involved with such countries, which suggests that more investment would be preferable. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text indicates that natural-resource extraction requires substantial initial investments (to acquire things like required technologies) and that there are fewer likely investors willing to participate at this stage than one might think, the text does not address what investors are likely to do over time as the industry stabilizes itself.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323036,"updateDate":1730147323036}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323036, 1730147323036),
    ($SATQ$714e4c10$SATQ$, $SATQ$b07b52e9-c1ee-4177-bce8-9a5b35a6f12f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ruth Asawa was an accomplished artist who worked in many art forms, including her unique tied-wire sculptures, but she was dedicated to more than the creation of art. Asawa also wanted to bring art to children in her hometown of San Francisco, California. To that end, in 1968 she cofounded the Alvarado School Arts Workshop, which brought works of art and artists into public schools, and in 1982 she helped found a San Francisco public arts high school, which was later named after her.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"29a44ddd-2ef7-4c59-b31a-4697d318cf69","content":"<p>Asawa inspired many other artists to share their work with students in public schools.</p>"},{"id":"dfd0fbea-13a9-4dd4-a30f-4be87524b6c3","content":"<p>Asawa&rsquo;s unique sculptures were appreciated more by local art communities than they were nationwide.</p>"},{"id":"c3c8c0ca-64d6-4b9f-bb90-4ff502da634d","content":"<p>Asawa&rsquo;s interest in art education prompted her to create art programs for students in San Francisco.</p>"},{"id":"fbf814fb-fe85-4811-b030-e5e1c26441b1","content":"<p>Asawa left a promising career as a sculptor to work as an art teacher in San Francisco schools.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c3c8c0ca-64d6-4b9f-bb90-4ff502da634d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text begins by establishing Ruth Asawa as an artist who worked in several art forms but emphasizes that she was interested in art for reasons other than her desire to create it. The text mentions two ways in which Asawa brought art to children through public schools: cofounding the Alvarado School Arts Workshop in 1968 and helping found a public arts high school in 1982. Thus, the main idea is that Asawa&rsquo;s interest in art education motivated her to establish arts programs for students in San Francisco.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention Asawa inspiring other artists to share their work with students. While the text does note that the Alvarado School Arts Workshop brought works of art and artists into public schools, it doesn&rsquo;t state that Asawa motivated these artists. Choice B is incorrect because the text makes no comparison between local and national appreciation of Asawa&rsquo;s sculptures. While the text does mention that Asawa created unique tied-wire sculptures and was connected to San Francisco, it does not contrast her local and national critical receptions. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text emphasizes Asawa&rsquo;s educational initiatives, it doesn&rsquo;t state or imply that she abandoned her career as a sculptor to work as an art teacher. Instead, the text portrays her educational work as a complement to her career as a successful artist.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"714e4c10","external_id":"b07b52e9-c1ee-4177-bce8-9a5b35a6f12f","vaultid":null,"uId":"0e688f6e-457b-4aa2-af5e-d8cf91a6d906","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ruth Asawa was an accomplished artist who worked in many art forms, including her unique tied-wire sculptures, but she was dedicated to more than the creation of art. Asawa also wanted to bring art to children in her hometown of San Francisco, California. To that end, in 1968 she cofounded the Alvarado School Arts Workshop, which brought works of art and artists into public schools, and in 1982 she helped found a San Francisco public arts high school, which was later named after her.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"29a44ddd-2ef7-4c59-b31a-4697d318cf69","content":"<p>Asawa inspired many other artists to share their work with students in public schools.</p>"},{"id":"dfd0fbea-13a9-4dd4-a30f-4be87524b6c3","content":"<p>Asawa&rsquo;s unique sculptures were appreciated more by local art communities than they were nationwide.</p>"},{"id":"c3c8c0ca-64d6-4b9f-bb90-4ff502da634d","content":"<p>Asawa&rsquo;s interest in art education prompted her to create art programs for students in San Francisco.</p>"},{"id":"fbf814fb-fe85-4811-b030-e5e1c26441b1","content":"<p>Asawa left a promising career as a sculptor to work as an art teacher in San Francisco schools.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["c3c8c0ca-64d6-4b9f-bb90-4ff502da634d"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text begins by establishing Ruth Asawa as an artist who worked in several art forms but emphasizes that she was interested in art for reasons other than her desire to create it. The text mentions two ways in which Asawa brought art to children through public schools: cofounding the Alvarado School Arts Workshop in 1968 and helping found a public arts high school in 1982. Thus, the main idea is that Asawa&rsquo;s interest in art education motivated her to establish arts programs for students in San Francisco.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention Asawa inspiring other artists to share their work with students. While the text does note that the Alvarado School Arts Workshop brought works of art and artists into public schools, it doesn&rsquo;t state that Asawa motivated these artists. Choice B is incorrect because the text makes no comparison between local and national appreciation of Asawa&rsquo;s sculptures. While the text does mention that Asawa created unique tied-wire sculptures and was connected to San Francisco, it does not contrast her local and national critical receptions. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text emphasizes Asawa&rsquo;s educational initiatives, it doesn&rsquo;t state or imply that she abandoned her career as a sculptor to work as an art teacher. Instead, the text portrays her educational work as a complement to her career as a successful artist.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$71904085$SATQ$, $SATQ$65d3f00d-f48c-4518-88b0-b5d58cbe4cf9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$06bfffc0-30ed-47b8-942f-c0ad0f483bc0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Linguist Deborah Tannen has cautioned against framing contentious issues in terms of two highly competitive perspectives, such as pro versus con. According to Tannen, this debate-driven approach can strip issues of their complexity and, when used in front of an audience, can be less informative than the presentation of multiple perspectives in a noncompetitive format. To test Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, students conducted a study in which they showed participants one of three different versions of local news commentary about the same issue. Each version featured a debate between two commentators with opposing views, a panel of three commentators with various views, or a single commentator.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding from the students&rsquo; study, if true, would most strongly support Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f8a49dc9-fe02-4fd7-9a39-acbcd4f13906","content":"<p>On average, participants perceived commentators in the debate as more knowledgeable about the issue than commentators in the panel.</p>"},{"id":"49072328-d1de-40f3-88ba-09be3f56c560","content":"<p>On average, participants perceived commentators in the panel as more knowledgeable about the issue than the single commentator.</p>"},{"id":"18ebb0f3-d5b0-40b0-b69d-6be94efb6577","content":"<p>On average, participants who watched the panel correctly answered more questions about the issue than those who watched the debate or the single commentator did.</p>"},{"id":"08a9d356-ec5a-4ce0-a608-a199c0ae6ccc","content":"<p>On average, participants who watched the single commentator correctly answered more questions about the issue than those who watched the debate did.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["18ebb0f3-d5b0-40b0-b69d-6be94efb6577"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents the finding that, if true, would most strongly support Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis. According to the text, Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis is that multiple perspectives presented in a noncompetitive format is more informative than a debate between opposing viewpoints is. If participants who saw a panel of three commentators with various views about an issue answered more questions about the issue correctly than did participants who saw a debate, that would support Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis since it would show that participants who heard multiple varied perspectives were better informed than were participants who heard a debate between opposing viewpoints.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that participants perceived commentators in the debate as more knowledgeable than commentators in the panel is irrelevant to Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, which is that presenting multiple perspectives on an issue is more informative to the audience than presenting opposing views of the issue is. Participants&rsquo; perception of how knowledgeable panelists are has no bearing on how much participants learn from the panelists. Choice B is incorrect because finding that participants perceived commentators in the panel as more knowledgeable than a single commentator is irrelevant to Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, which is that presenting multiple perspectives on an issue is more informative to the audience than presenting opposing views of the issue is. Participants&rsquo; perception of how knowledgeable panelists are has no bearing on how much participants learn from the panelists, and Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis says nothing about how informative single commentators are. Choice D is incorrect because finding that participants who watched a single commentator answered more questions correctly than participants who watched the debate did wouldn&rsquo;t be relevant to Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, which is that hearing multiple varying perspectives is more informative than hearing a debate. Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis says nothing about how informative single commentators are.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-322-TXT$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1be32db7-d7a5-4003-85e8-97a481bf7c56$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"71904085","external_id":"65d3f00d-f48c-4518-88b0-b5d58cbe4cf9","vaultid":"06bfffc0-30ed-47b8-942f-c0ad0f483bc0","uId":"1745307a-9f51-42b2-bf1f-dd3320ee9e88","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Linguist Deborah Tannen has cautioned against framing contentious issues in terms of two highly competitive perspectives, such as pro versus con. According to Tannen, this debate-driven approach can strip issues of their complexity and, when used in front of an audience, can be less informative than the presentation of multiple perspectives in a noncompetitive format. To test Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, students conducted a study in which they showed participants one of three different versions of local news commentary about the same issue. Each version featured a debate between two commentators with opposing views, a panel of three commentators with various views, or a single commentator.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding from the students&rsquo; study, if true, would most strongly support Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f8a49dc9-fe02-4fd7-9a39-acbcd4f13906","content":"<p>On average, participants perceived commentators in the debate as more knowledgeable about the issue than commentators in the panel.</p>"},{"id":"49072328-d1de-40f3-88ba-09be3f56c560","content":"<p>On average, participants perceived commentators in the panel as more knowledgeable about the issue than the single commentator.</p>"},{"id":"18ebb0f3-d5b0-40b0-b69d-6be94efb6577","content":"<p>On average, participants who watched the panel correctly answered more questions about the issue than those who watched the debate or the single commentator did.</p>"},{"id":"08a9d356-ec5a-4ce0-a608-a199c0ae6ccc","content":"<p>On average, participants who watched the single commentator correctly answered more questions about the issue than those who watched the debate did.</p>"}],"keys":["18ebb0f3-d5b0-40b0-b69d-6be94efb6577"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents the finding that, if true, would most strongly support Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis. According to the text, Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis is that multiple perspectives presented in a noncompetitive format is more informative than a debate between opposing viewpoints is. If participants who saw a panel of three commentators with various views about an issue answered more questions about the issue correctly than did participants who saw a debate, that would support Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis since it would show that participants who heard multiple varied perspectives were better informed than were participants who heard a debate between opposing viewpoints.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that participants perceived commentators in the debate as more knowledgeable than commentators in the panel is irrelevant to Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, which is that presenting multiple perspectives on an issue is more informative to the audience than presenting opposing views of the issue is. Participants&rsquo; perception of how knowledgeable panelists are has no bearing on how much participants learn from the panelists. Choice B is incorrect because finding that participants perceived commentators in the panel as more knowledgeable than a single commentator is irrelevant to Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, which is that presenting multiple perspectives on an issue is more informative to the audience than presenting opposing views of the issue is. Participants&rsquo; perception of how knowledgeable panelists are has no bearing on how much participants learn from the panelists, and Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis says nothing about how informative single commentators are. Choice D is incorrect because finding that participants who watched a single commentator answered more questions correctly than participants who watched the debate did wouldn&rsquo;t be relevant to Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis, which is that hearing multiple varying perspectives is more informative than hearing a debate. Tannen&rsquo;s hypothesis says nothing about how informative single commentators are.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-322-TXT","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1be32db7-d7a5-4003-85e8-97a481bf7c56","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959335,"updateDate":1691007959335}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959335, 1691007959335),
    ($SATQ$7254379e$SATQ$, $SATQ$22a7e235-ee44-4c74-bca9-5b5d9a61b01a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b745051a-a19c-4f28-9f95-7b58484f3b4d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Some residents in a neighborhood in Atlanta recently founded a community garden inside a local park. The residents agreed to volunteer to take care of the garden together. Students at a local high school surveyed some of the volunteers as part of a project to understand the impact of the new garden. The students concluded that the new garden benefited the community overall by fostering connections and relationships between the volunteers and other residents of the neighborhood who weren&rsquo;t volunteering at the garden.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from a survey respondent would best illustrate the students&rsquo; conclusion?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e6be1872-5a28-4b67-afd3-98de5551248e","content":"<p>&ldquo;Our first challenge was deciding what plants would be most suitable to the climate and soil here in Atlanta. We needed plants that could survive the hot and humid summers.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"dc6e99f8-579c-4c1d-adb3-edc3802a066c","content":"<p>&ldquo;We&rsquo;re lucky to have a few expert gardeners living in the neighborhood. Some volunteers and I have gone to them a few times with questions, and they&rsquo;ve been eager to help us and to learn more about the project.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"04b19451-3bd3-4020-a94e-9f16444099f1","content":"<p>&ldquo;I love getting the opportunity to be outside and around nature, especially on days when the weather is nice.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"0297318d-003a-41cb-83ac-2ab828b6511e","content":"<p>&ldquo;My favorite thing about the garden is the feeling of pride I get when I walk by each day. As I see the plants growing, I feel good knowing I had a small part in creating this beautiful space in the neighborhood.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dc6e99f8-579c-4c1d-adb3-edc3802a066c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice shows how volunteers have been interacting with nonvolunteer neighbors and benefiting from their gardening expertise: proof of the connections enabled by the garden.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This volunteer describes the challenges of developing the garden, which isn&rsquo;t connected to the conclusion about the garden fostering connections between volunteers and neighbors. Choice C is incorrect. While the volunteer expresses enthusiasm for the garden, they don&rsquo;t mention any interactions with other people, which is what the students need to show for their conclusion. Choice D is incorrect. The respondent enjoys and takes pride in the garden, but they don&rsquo;t mention interacting with neighbors or other volunteers.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-E007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0af3cc20-4926-4411-a054-b00e9e432915$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7254379e","external_id":"22a7e235-ee44-4c74-bca9-5b5d9a61b01a","vaultid":"b745051a-a19c-4f28-9f95-7b58484f3b4d","uId":"d0a524d8-9b9b-4ea1-b8ec-2945c400a734","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Some residents in a neighborhood in Atlanta recently founded a community garden inside a local park. The residents agreed to volunteer to take care of the garden together. Students at a local high school surveyed some of the volunteers as part of a project to understand the impact of the new garden. The students concluded that the new garden benefited the community overall by fostering connections and relationships between the volunteers and other residents of the neighborhood who weren&rsquo;t volunteering at the garden.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from a survey respondent would best illustrate the students&rsquo; conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e6be1872-5a28-4b67-afd3-98de5551248e","content":"<p>&ldquo;Our first challenge was deciding what plants would be most suitable to the climate and soil here in Atlanta. We needed plants that could survive the hot and humid summers.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"dc6e99f8-579c-4c1d-adb3-edc3802a066c","content":"<p>&ldquo;We&rsquo;re lucky to have a few expert gardeners living in the neighborhood. Some volunteers and I have gone to them a few times with questions, and they&rsquo;ve been eager to help us and to learn more about the project.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"04b19451-3bd3-4020-a94e-9f16444099f1","content":"<p>&ldquo;I love getting the opportunity to be outside and around nature, especially on days when the weather is nice.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"0297318d-003a-41cb-83ac-2ab828b6511e","content":"<p>&ldquo;My favorite thing about the garden is the feeling of pride I get when I walk by each day. As I see the plants growing, I feel good knowing I had a small part in creating this beautiful space in the neighborhood.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["dc6e99f8-579c-4c1d-adb3-edc3802a066c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice shows how volunteers have been interacting with nonvolunteer neighbors and benefiting from their gardening expertise: proof of the connections enabled by the garden.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This volunteer describes the challenges of developing the garden, which isn&rsquo;t connected to the conclusion about the garden fostering connections between volunteers and neighbors. Choice C is incorrect. While the volunteer expresses enthusiasm for the garden, they don&rsquo;t mention any interactions with other people, which is what the students need to show for their conclusion. Choice D is incorrect. The respondent enjoys and takes pride in the garden, but they don&rsquo;t mention interacting with neighbors or other volunteers.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-E007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0af3cc20-4926-4411-a054-b00e9e432915","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959376,"updateDate":1691007959376}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959376, 1691007959376),
    ($SATQ$72c7dafd$SATQ$, $SATQ$6a7567bd-5e99-4a4d-9a43-78b9caf58754$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>Happy House</em> is a 1920 novel by Jane Abbott. The narrator presents a young woman as being unimpressed with the house she is visiting: <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from <em>Happy House</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3085293d-7665-442b-bf9c-6ea581895790","content":"<p>&ldquo;Her first feeling was of disappointment; in the square lines of the house there was little claim to beauty.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"ee7d5b64-4307-49b7-b5a3-10ff4017b8cb","content":"<p>&ldquo;Someone had opened one of the blinds so here there was more light.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"350f5084-90a4-486a-a55a-c0f4caee091d","content":"<p>&ldquo;The door, built squarely in the middle of the house, opened almost directly upon a stone-flagged path that led in a straight line to the road.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"3e58a27c-dcbe-46da-b8c4-6ddbc9c60516","content":"<p>&ldquo;She tip-toed through the hall and opened the door on the right.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3085293d-7665-442b-bf9c-6ea581895790"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses a quotation from <em>Happy House</em> to illustrate the claim that the young woman is unimpressed with the house she is visiting. In the quotation, it says the young woman&rsquo;s "first feeling was of disappointment" and that "there was little claim to beauty" when referring to the house. This disappointment and the overall lack of beauty in the house suggest that the young woman isn&rsquo;t impressed by the house she is visiting.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t describe what the young woman thinks about the house. Instead, it indicates that there is more light because window blinds have been opened. Choice C is incorrect because the quotation describes a door and a path outside of the house but doesn&rsquo;t include anything to indicate the young woman&rsquo;s feelings about the house. Choice D is incorrect because the quotation states what the young woman did in the house ("tip-toed through the hall" and "opened the door"), but it doesn&rsquo;t show what she thinks about it.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"72c7dafd","external_id":"6a7567bd-5e99-4a4d-9a43-78b9caf58754","vaultid":null,"uId":"e4110489-ea1b-4189-89da-101369eefa97","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>Happy House</em> is a 1920 novel by Jane Abbott. The narrator presents a young woman as being unimpressed with the house she is visiting: <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from <em>Happy House</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3085293d-7665-442b-bf9c-6ea581895790","content":"<p>&ldquo;Her first feeling was of disappointment; in the square lines of the house there was little claim to beauty.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"ee7d5b64-4307-49b7-b5a3-10ff4017b8cb","content":"<p>&ldquo;Someone had opened one of the blinds so here there was more light.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"350f5084-90a4-486a-a55a-c0f4caee091d","content":"<p>&ldquo;The door, built squarely in the middle of the house, opened almost directly upon a stone-flagged path that led in a straight line to the road.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"3e58a27c-dcbe-46da-b8c4-6ddbc9c60516","content":"<p>&ldquo;She tip-toed through the hall and opened the door on the right.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["3085293d-7665-442b-bf9c-6ea581895790"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses a quotation from <em>Happy House</em> to illustrate the claim that the young woman is unimpressed with the house she is visiting. In the quotation, it says the young woman&rsquo;s \"first feeling was of disappointment\" and that \"there was little claim to beauty\" when referring to the house. This disappointment and the overall lack of beauty in the house suggest that the young woman isn&rsquo;t impressed by the house she is visiting.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t describe what the young woman thinks about the house. Instead, it indicates that there is more light because window blinds have been opened. Choice C is incorrect because the quotation describes a door and a path outside of the house but doesn&rsquo;t include anything to indicate the young woman&rsquo;s feelings about the house. Choice D is incorrect because the quotation states what the young woman did in the house (\"tip-toed through the hall\" and \"opened the door\"), but it doesn&rsquo;t show what she thinks about it.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323045,"updateDate":1730147323045}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323045, 1730147323045),
    ($SATQ$72cbdbc6$SATQ$, $SATQ$f6a00237-7406-4f9c-a897-4e706b7fdb72$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Researchers Eugeni Vidal-Tortosa and Robin Lovelace looked at the relationship between street lighting in a city and people&rsquo;s willingness to ride a bicycle. Their results suggest that poor street lighting can deter new or inexperienced cyclists from riding in a city but has little effect on experienced cyclists. Therefore, increasing the number of streetlights in a city could potentially <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"961d1d34-c41a-489c-b891-16cd64641593","content":"<p>decrease the number of new or inexperienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"},{"id":"6f4dec3c-10ba-483d-bd5f-ed9ca788d86a","content":"<p>increase the number of experienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"},{"id":"0771d29f-a019-4db8-b55e-fb7afabfa1ef","content":"<p>decrease the number of experienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"},{"id":"ead716d3-9641-4cca-a00f-1de035b3ca84","content":"<p>increase the number of new or inexperienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ead716d3-9641-4cca-a00f-1de035b3ca84"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the relationship between street lighting and people&rsquo;s cycling behavior. According to the text, Eugeni Vidal-Tortosa and Robin Lovelace found that poor street lighting deters new or inexperienced cyclists from riding their bikes in a city but has little effect on experienced cyclists. This finding establishes a causal relationship between street lighting and the willingness of new or inexperienced cyclists to ride a bicycle in the city: if poor lighting discourages new or inexperienced cyclists from riding, then improving lighting conditions by increasing the number of streetlights would logically remove this deterrent, potentially leading to an increase in the number of new or inexperienced cyclists willing to ride in the city.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it contradicts the logical relationship established in the text. If poor street lighting deters new or inexperienced cyclists from riding in the city, then increasing the number of streetlights (improving lighting conditions) would be expected to encourage more of these cyclists to ride, not decrease their numbers. Choice B is incorrect because the text explicitly states that street lighting didn&rsquo;t have an effect on experienced cyclists. If lighting conditions don&rsquo;t significantly influence experienced cyclists&rsquo; decisions to ride, then increasing the number of streetlights wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily increase the number of experienced cyclists riding in the city. Choice C is incorrect. The text indicates that street lighting didn&rsquo;t affect experienced cyclists, so increasing the number of streetlights wouldn&rsquo;t decrease the participation of these cyclists. Instead, their numbers would be expected to remain about the same.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"72cbdbc6","external_id":"f6a00237-7406-4f9c-a897-4e706b7fdb72","vaultid":null,"uId":"933c9f33-0a63-47d3-9572-a86e04da1e4e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Researchers Eugeni Vidal-Tortosa and Robin Lovelace looked at the relationship between street lighting in a city and people&rsquo;s willingness to ride a bicycle. Their results suggest that poor street lighting can deter new or inexperienced cyclists from riding in a city but has little effect on experienced cyclists. Therefore, increasing the number of streetlights in a city could potentially <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"961d1d34-c41a-489c-b891-16cd64641593","content":"<p>decrease the number of new or inexperienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"},{"id":"6f4dec3c-10ba-483d-bd5f-ed9ca788d86a","content":"<p>increase the number of experienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"},{"id":"0771d29f-a019-4db8-b55e-fb7afabfa1ef","content":"<p>decrease the number of experienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"},{"id":"ead716d3-9641-4cca-a00f-1de035b3ca84","content":"<p>increase the number of new or inexperienced cyclists riding in the city.</p>"}],"keys":["ead716d3-9641-4cca-a00f-1de035b3ca84"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the relationship between street lighting and people&rsquo;s cycling behavior. According to the text, Eugeni Vidal-Tortosa and Robin Lovelace found that poor street lighting deters new or inexperienced cyclists from riding their bikes in a city but has little effect on experienced cyclists. This finding establishes a causal relationship between street lighting and the willingness of new or inexperienced cyclists to ride a bicycle in the city: if poor lighting discourages new or inexperienced cyclists from riding, then improving lighting conditions by increasing the number of streetlights would logically remove this deterrent, potentially leading to an increase in the number of new or inexperienced cyclists willing to ride in the city.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it contradicts the logical relationship established in the text. If poor street lighting deters new or inexperienced cyclists from riding in the city, then increasing the number of streetlights (improving lighting conditions) would be expected to encourage more of these cyclists to ride, not decrease their numbers. Choice B is incorrect because the text explicitly states that street lighting didn&rsquo;t have an effect on experienced cyclists. If lighting conditions don&rsquo;t significantly influence experienced cyclists&rsquo; decisions to ride, then increasing the number of streetlights wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily increase the number of experienced cyclists riding in the city. Choice C is incorrect. The text indicates that street lighting didn&rsquo;t affect experienced cyclists, so increasing the number of streetlights wouldn&rsquo;t decrease the participation of these cyclists. Instead, their numbers would be expected to remain about the same.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771171,"updateDate":1776362771171}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771171, 1776362771171),
    ($SATQ$73c091d2$SATQ$, $SATQ$7be53a85-0e20-4c88-9079-9002b68979fa$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Many trees produce growth rings as they age, with each ring in a tree&rsquo;s trunk representing one year in the tree&rsquo;s life. This often makes it fairly easy to determine how old a tree was when it was cut down. To do so, you look at the tree stump and count the dark rings you see. But a researcher claims that this method often can&rsquo;t be used to identify the age of olive trees.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which detail, if true, would most directly support the researcher&rsquo;s claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ac0b90c8-6c1c-4783-ba2c-f0875cf644cb","content":"<p>The oldest olive tree in the world is likely over 1,100 years old.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"07d4d63d-37dc-4ce7-b4d8-3ce09f94ef17","content":"<p>Narrow growth rings can suggest that an olive tree experienced harsh conditions.</p>"},{"id":"a5baf2ce-fd03-4920-beed-cffb77263395","content":"<p>Many olive trees have growth rings that are difficult to see.</p>"},{"id":"00ac4ed8-db6b-4451-b1ff-9cfa3fb9539b","content":"<p>Olive trees thrive in areas with hot, dry summers.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a5baf2ce-fd03-4920-beed-cffb77263395"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a detail that, if true, would most directly support the researcher&rsquo;s claim about the difficulty of determining olive tree age by ring counting. The text explains that for many trees, counting the dark rings visible in a tree stump is a reliable method for determining the tree&rsquo;s age. However, if olive trees have growth rings that are difficult to see, it would be hard to count the individual rings. Therefore, the ring-counting method would be challenging or impossible to use for olive trees. This directly supports the researcher&rsquo;s claim that the ring-counting method often can&rsquo;t be used for olive trees because it identifies a specific characteristic of olive trees that could render this approach impracticable.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the probable age of the oldest olive tree doesn&rsquo;t explain why counting rings wouldn&rsquo;t work for determining olive tree age. The older age of some olive trees might make counting more tedious but doesn&rsquo;t pose an insurmountable obstacle to adopting the method. The researcher&rsquo;s claim is about the feasibility of the ring-counting method for olive trees, not about the potential impracticality of employing this method for particularly long-lived trees. Choice B is incorrect because the fact that narrow growth rings might indicate harsh growing conditions has no direct bearing on the claim that counting rings often can&rsquo;t be used to determine olive tree age. It is possible that growth rings that are too narrowly spaced might be difficult to count, but the researcher&rsquo;s claim relates to the inapplicability of the ring-counting method to olive trees in general rather than to olive trees that have experienced harsh conditions. Moreover, as long as they are visible, even narrow growth rings might permit the ring-counting method to be used to determine tree age. Choice D is incorrect because the ideal climate conditions for olive trees don&rsquo;t explain why counting rings wouldn&rsquo;t work as a method for determining their age. While growing conditions might affect ring formation patterns, the simple fact that olive trees thrive in hot, dry environments doesn&rsquo;t directly address why the conventional method of counting rings would be ineffective for determining their age.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"73c091d2","external_id":"7be53a85-0e20-4c88-9079-9002b68979fa","vaultid":null,"uId":"c29f0216-5b2f-4fee-8848-6fa50eedc39c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Many trees produce growth rings as they age, with each ring in a tree&rsquo;s trunk representing one year in the tree&rsquo;s life. This often makes it fairly easy to determine how old a tree was when it was cut down. To do so, you look at the tree stump and count the dark rings you see. But a researcher claims that this method often can&rsquo;t be used to identify the age of olive trees.</p>","stem":"<p>Which detail, if true, would most directly support the researcher&rsquo;s claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ac0b90c8-6c1c-4783-ba2c-f0875cf644cb","content":"<p>The oldest olive tree in the world is likely over 1,100 years old.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"07d4d63d-37dc-4ce7-b4d8-3ce09f94ef17","content":"<p>Narrow growth rings can suggest that an olive tree experienced harsh conditions.</p>"},{"id":"a5baf2ce-fd03-4920-beed-cffb77263395","content":"<p>Many olive trees have growth rings that are difficult to see.</p>"},{"id":"00ac4ed8-db6b-4451-b1ff-9cfa3fb9539b","content":"<p>Olive trees thrive in areas with hot, dry summers.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["a5baf2ce-fd03-4920-beed-cffb77263395"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a detail that, if true, would most directly support the researcher&rsquo;s claim about the difficulty of determining olive tree age by ring counting. The text explains that for many trees, counting the dark rings visible in a tree stump is a reliable method for determining the tree&rsquo;s age. However, if olive trees have growth rings that are difficult to see, it would be hard to count the individual rings. Therefore, the ring-counting method would be challenging or impossible to use for olive trees. This directly supports the researcher&rsquo;s claim that the ring-counting method often can&rsquo;t be used for olive trees because it identifies a specific characteristic of olive trees that could render this approach impracticable.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the probable age of the oldest olive tree doesn&rsquo;t explain why counting rings wouldn&rsquo;t work for determining olive tree age. The older age of some olive trees might make counting more tedious but doesn&rsquo;t pose an insurmountable obstacle to adopting the method. The researcher&rsquo;s claim is about the feasibility of the ring-counting method for olive trees, not about the potential impracticality of employing this method for particularly long-lived trees. Choice B is incorrect because the fact that narrow growth rings might indicate harsh growing conditions has no direct bearing on the claim that counting rings often can&rsquo;t be used to determine olive tree age. It is possible that growth rings that are too narrowly spaced might be difficult to count, but the researcher&rsquo;s claim relates to the inapplicability of the ring-counting method to olive trees in general rather than to olive trees that have experienced harsh conditions. Moreover, as long as they are visible, even narrow growth rings might permit the ring-counting method to be used to determine tree age. Choice D is incorrect because the ideal climate conditions for olive trees don&rsquo;t explain why counting rings wouldn&rsquo;t work as a method for determining their age. While growing conditions might affect ring formation patterns, the simple fact that olive trees thrive in hot, dry environments doesn&rsquo;t directly address why the conventional method of counting rings would be ineffective for determining their age.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061178,"updateDate":1755117061178}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061178, 1755117061178),
    ($SATQ$73caa3ab$SATQ$, $SATQ$d6a68288-df99-46df-af06-1d642b22ed89$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Mark Haber&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as="date">2022</span> novel <em>Saint Sebastian&rsquo;s Abyss</em>. The narrator and Schmidt are both art critics.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When my first wife admitted to Schmidt over dinner that she didn&rsquo;t find art, painting in particular, especially compelling, Schmidt winced, set down his fork, and sighed dramatically; he then excused himself, explaining an appointment he&rsquo;d forgotten about had suddenly and inexplicably been remembered, while making it abundantly clear there was no appointment at all.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;<span data-ssml-say-as="date">2022</span> by Mark Haber</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the text, what is notable about Schmidt&rsquo;s behavior?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ea0272d4-0760-4dfd-9667-33c8ceb26010","content":"<p>Schmidt is only given to theatrical behavior when in the company of the narrator and his first wife.</p>"},{"id":"d557f84f-04eb-43fd-82f5-426d0e506511","content":"<p>Schmidt&rsquo;s absentmindedness regarding his schedule is uncharacteristic of him.</p>"},{"id":"bcd68876-1bee-4f40-8187-7529b46d88e3","content":"<p>Schmidt&rsquo;s departure is occasioned by the resumption of a previous disagreement with the narrator&rsquo;s first wife about a particular painting.</p>"},{"id":"30f29273-b40d-4dee-be13-ca18c64a7795","content":"<p>Schmidt conveys his feelings about one of his dining companions without explicitly stating them.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["30f29273-b40d-4dee-be13-ca18c64a7795"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes what is notable about Schmidt&rsquo;s behavior. According to the text, when the narrator&rsquo;s first wife admits she doesn&rsquo;t find art particularly compelling, Schmidt responds by wincing, sighing dramatically, and abruptly leaving dinner with an obviously false excuse about a remembered appointment. The narrator makes it clear that there is no actual appointment and that the excuse is merely a pretense. Through these actions&mdash;the physical reactions of wincing and sighing, followed by the transparent excuse to leave&mdash;Schmidt clearly communicates his disapproval of the wife without directly stating his feelings about her.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text provides no evidence that Schmidt&rsquo;s theatrical behavior&mdash;wincing, sighing dramatically, and abruptly leaving dinner&mdash;is unique to situations involving the narrator and his first wife. The text describes only one interaction involving Schmidt, so there&rsquo;s no basis for determining whether his dramatic reactions are typical of him in all situations or limited to this particular social context. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Schmidt is typically mindful of his schedule or that forgetting appointments would be uncharacteristic of him. Rather, the text makes it clear that Schmidt hasn&rsquo;t actually forgotten an appointment at all&mdash;the narrator indicates that Schmidt&rsquo;s reason for leaving is an obvious fabrication, not a genuine instance of absentmindedness. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention any previous disagreement between Schmidt and the narrator&rsquo;s first wife about a particular painting or any other topic. The text presents what appears to be a spontaneous reaction by Schmidt to the wife&rsquo;s statement about not finding art compelling, not the continuation of an earlier conversation or dispute.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"73caa3ab","external_id":"d6a68288-df99-46df-af06-1d642b22ed89","vaultid":null,"uId":"a1ac9c1b-9ac4-4a85-b6f3-b1d452560fce","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Mark Haber&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2022</span> novel <em>Saint Sebastian&rsquo;s Abyss</em>. The narrator and Schmidt are both art critics.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When my first wife admitted to Schmidt over dinner that she didn&rsquo;t find art, painting in particular, especially compelling, Schmidt winced, set down his fork, and sighed dramatically; he then excused himself, explaining an appointment he&rsquo;d forgotten about had suddenly and inexplicably been remembered, while making it abundantly clear there was no appointment at all.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;<span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2022</span> by Mark Haber</span></p>","stem":"<p>Based on the text, what is notable about Schmidt&rsquo;s behavior?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ea0272d4-0760-4dfd-9667-33c8ceb26010","content":"<p>Schmidt is only given to theatrical behavior when in the company of the narrator and his first wife.</p>"},{"id":"d557f84f-04eb-43fd-82f5-426d0e506511","content":"<p>Schmidt&rsquo;s absentmindedness regarding his schedule is uncharacteristic of him.</p>"},{"id":"bcd68876-1bee-4f40-8187-7529b46d88e3","content":"<p>Schmidt&rsquo;s departure is occasioned by the resumption of a previous disagreement with the narrator&rsquo;s first wife about a particular painting.</p>"},{"id":"30f29273-b40d-4dee-be13-ca18c64a7795","content":"<p>Schmidt conveys his feelings about one of his dining companions without explicitly stating them.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["30f29273-b40d-4dee-be13-ca18c64a7795"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes what is notable about Schmidt&rsquo;s behavior. According to the text, when the narrator&rsquo;s first wife admits she doesn&rsquo;t find art particularly compelling, Schmidt responds by wincing, sighing dramatically, and abruptly leaving dinner with an obviously false excuse about a remembered appointment. The narrator makes it clear that there is no actual appointment and that the excuse is merely a pretense. Through these actions&mdash;the physical reactions of wincing and sighing, followed by the transparent excuse to leave&mdash;Schmidt clearly communicates his disapproval of the wife without directly stating his feelings about her.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text provides no evidence that Schmidt&rsquo;s theatrical behavior&mdash;wincing, sighing dramatically, and abruptly leaving dinner&mdash;is unique to situations involving the narrator and his first wife. The text describes only one interaction involving Schmidt, so there&rsquo;s no basis for determining whether his dramatic reactions are typical of him in all situations or limited to this particular social context. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Schmidt is typically mindful of his schedule or that forgetting appointments would be uncharacteristic of him. Rather, the text makes it clear that Schmidt hasn&rsquo;t actually forgotten an appointment at all&mdash;the narrator indicates that Schmidt&rsquo;s reason for leaving is an obvious fabrication, not a genuine instance of absentmindedness. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention any previous disagreement between Schmidt and the narrator&rsquo;s first wife about a particular painting or any other topic. The text presents what appears to be a spontaneous reaction by Schmidt to the wife&rsquo;s statement about not finding art compelling, not the continuation of an earlier conversation or dispute.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$73d457b6$SATQ$, $SATQ$56f276d0-7381-4372-865d-9872b5654569$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the 1970s, a roughly 60,000-year-old piece of hyena bone marked with nine notches was discovered at a site in western France once inhabited by Neanderthals. Although many believe that only modern humans developed systems for notating numbers, one archaeologist asserts that this artifact may be a sign that Neanderthals also recorded numerical information. The notches on the bone are unevenly spaced but approximately parallel, and microscopic analysis reveals that they were made with a single stone tool; according to the archaeologist, <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">this suggests that the notches were all made at one time by one individual as a means of counting something.</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the underlined claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dc0c3ee6-ed78-4d88-8d60-ccd587c2a830","content":"<p>Parallel lines are a common feature in modern humans&rsquo; early systems for recording numerical information.</p>"},{"id":"c62b0ed4-00e8-479a-8a75-6672fe29a75e","content":"<p>More than nine approximately parallel notches made with a different stone tool are present on another artifact found at a site in western France.</p>"},{"id":"f9650f1f-8f5e-43c1-aa82-ae88c7c4ee37","content":"<p>It would have taken careful effort to make evenly spaced lines on bone with the stone tools typically used by Neanderthals.</p>"},{"id":"92296773-62e1-48ea-ae9d-faea63b2f071","content":"<p>Decorative art discovered at another Neanderthal site in western France primarily features patterns of unevenly spaced parallel lines.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["92296773-62e1-48ea-ae9d-faea63b2f071"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The archeologist bases their claim on the fact that the hyena bone features unevenly spaced parallel notches. But if unevenly spaced parallel lines were found on "decorative art" at another Neanderthal site, it would suggest that the hyena bone is probably decorative art as well&mdash;not a counting tool.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t weaken the underlined claim. If anything, it might actually strengthen the claim: assuming we can make an inference about Neanderthals using a fact about early humans, it provides more support for the idea that the person who made the notches was counting something. Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t weaken the underlined claim. The fact that another artifact had parallel notches made with a different stone tool doesn&rsquo;t tell us anything about the use of either artifact. For all we know, they could both have been used for counting. Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t weaken the underlined claim. The fact that it took effort to make the lines doesn&rsquo;t tell us anything about what the lines were for.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"73d457b6","external_id":"56f276d0-7381-4372-865d-9872b5654569","vaultid":null,"uId":"eb6c723c-ef36-4703-8033-5350687819ed","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the 1970s, a roughly 60,000-year-old piece of hyena bone marked with nine notches was discovered at a site in western France once inhabited by Neanderthals. Although many believe that only modern humans developed systems for notating numbers, one archaeologist asserts that this artifact may be a sign that Neanderthals also recorded numerical information. The notches on the bone are unevenly spaced but approximately parallel, and microscopic analysis reveals that they were made with a single stone tool; according to the archaeologist, <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">this suggests that the notches were all made at one time by one individual as a means of counting something.</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the underlined claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"dc0c3ee6-ed78-4d88-8d60-ccd587c2a830","content":"<p>Parallel lines are a common feature in modern humans&rsquo; early systems for recording numerical information.</p>"},{"id":"c62b0ed4-00e8-479a-8a75-6672fe29a75e","content":"<p>More than nine approximately parallel notches made with a different stone tool are present on another artifact found at a site in western France.</p>"},{"id":"f9650f1f-8f5e-43c1-aa82-ae88c7c4ee37","content":"<p>It would have taken careful effort to make evenly spaced lines on bone with the stone tools typically used by Neanderthals.</p>"},{"id":"92296773-62e1-48ea-ae9d-faea63b2f071","content":"<p>Decorative art discovered at another Neanderthal site in western France primarily features patterns of unevenly spaced parallel lines.</p>"}],"keys":["92296773-62e1-48ea-ae9d-faea63b2f071"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The archeologist bases their claim on the fact that the hyena bone features unevenly spaced parallel notches. But if unevenly spaced parallel lines were found on \"decorative art\" at another Neanderthal site, it would suggest that the hyena bone is probably decorative art as well&mdash;not a counting tool.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t weaken the underlined claim. If anything, it might actually strengthen the claim: assuming we can make an inference about Neanderthals using a fact about early humans, it provides more support for the idea that the person who made the notches was counting something. Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t weaken the underlined claim. The fact that another artifact had parallel notches made with a different stone tool doesn&rsquo;t tell us anything about the use of either artifact. For all we know, they could both have been used for counting. Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t weaken the underlined claim. The fact that it took effort to make the lines doesn&rsquo;t tell us anything about what the lines were for.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$742fd8ba$SATQ$, $SATQ$121f0267-8731-4483-8bac-45f2f405e799$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$19093fc9-936c-4668-9606-8639cdd786cf$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<figure class="image"><svg aria-label="Bar graph titled Metal Content of Plants with and without Kanamycin Exposure. The horizontal axis is labeled Experimental condition. 2 data categories are shown. The vertical axis is labeled Metal content, in parts per million. It ranges from 0 to 700 in increments of 100. Refer to long description." height="501.5167236328125" role="img" viewbox="0 0 380 501.5167236328125" width="380" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g data-name="Layer 1" id="ed420550-79eb-48d4-af01-cd27cdd08afd"><defs>
